This version avoids both a length variable and a dummy node. `link` points to the pointer that leads to the current node. Once the moving pointer reaches the end, `link` points directly to the target node's incoming link, so the target can be skipped.

The pointer-to-pointer detail also handles removing the head: when the target is the head, `link` still points to `head`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* removeNthFromEnd(ListNode* head, int n) {
        ListNode** link = &head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            fast = fast->next;
        }

        while (fast != nullptr) {
            fast = fast->next;
            link = &((*link)->next);
        }

        ListNode* removed = *link;
        *link = removed->next;
        delete removed;

        return head;
    }
};
```

Dry run on `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` with `n = 2`: after the gap is made, `fast` starts at `3`. As it moves to the end, `link` ends up pointing to `3->next`, which contains `4`. Replacing that link with `4->next` gives `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 5`.

An empty result is also valid when the input has one node and `n` is `1`. The list links are changed and the removed node is deleted. Time complexity is `O(L)` and extra space is `O(1)`.

A fixed gap between two pointers avoids calculating the length. The dummy node is placed before the head, so deleting the head is handled just like deleting any other node.

First move `fast` by `n` nodes. Then move both pointers until `fast` reaches the last node. At that point, `slow.next` is the node to skip.

