class Solution {
    public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, head);
        ListNode slow = dummy;
        ListNode fast = dummy;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            fast = fast.next;
        }

        while (fast.next != null) {
            fast = fast.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }

        slow.next = slow.next.next;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

Dry run with `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]` and `n = 2`: after the first move, `fast` is at `2`. When the loop ends, `slow` is at `3`, so its next node, `4`, is removed. The list links are changed in place. This takes `O(L)` time and `O(1)` extra space.

Instead of keeping only two pointers, this version stores every node in an array list. That makes the node before the target easy to locate with an index. It is not the space-saving choice, but the indexing is simple to check.

For a list of length `L`, the target is stored at index `L - n`. The node before it is either the dummy node or index `L - n - 1`.

