import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, head);
        List<ListNode> nodes = new ArrayList<>();
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            nodes.add(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        int previousIndex = nodes.size() - n - 1;
        ListNode previous = previousIndex < 0
                ? dummy
                : nodes.get(previousIndex);

        previous.next = previous.next.next;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

Dry run for `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, `n = 2`: the target index is `5 - 2 = 3`, so the target is `4` and the previous node is at index `2`, which is `3`. The list is modified by changing one `next` reference. Time complexity is `O(L)` and space complexity is `O(L)`.

Counting the list first keeps the position calculation easy. After finding the length, `length - n` tells where the target sits from the front. A dummy node lets the same link update work even when the first node is removed.

For `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` and `n = 2`, the target is at front index `5 - 2 = 3`, which is node `4`.

